04,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,9885,279,2629,315,279,1931,19703,315,400,87,61,19,481,220,23,15,87,481,220,18,21,284,220,15,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the sum of the real roots of $x^4 - 80x - 36 = 0.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"5d147915091346df36c0dffc72baa24b9e6ebc9d7e5f82e6e8af90e595cc1e89"},"seconds":54.92027306556702,"seed":1072638017664783999,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the sum of the real roots of the polynomial equation \\(x^4 - 80x - 36 = 0\\), we can proceed as follows:\n\n1. **Analyze the Polynomial**: We have the equation:\n   \\[\n   x^4 - 80x - 36 = 0\n   \\]\n\n2. **Check for Possible Symmetry or Special Roots**: Notice that the polynomial is not symmetric (i.e., it does not have the form \\(f(x) = f(-x)\\) or \\(f(x) = -f(-x)\\)). Hence, roots will not necessarily come in pairs of the form \\(x\\) and \\(-x\\).\n\n3. **Find Potential Real Roots by Estimation**: Let's test some simple values to see if we can find any real roots:\n   - For \\(x = 0\\):\n     \\[\n     0^4 - 80 \\cdot 0 - 36 = -36 \\quad \\text{(not a root)}\n     \\]\n   - For \\(x = 1\\):\n     \\[\n     1^4 - 80 \\cdot 1 - 36 = 1 - 80 - 36 = -115 \\quad \\text{(not a root)}\n     \\]\n   - For \\(x = -1\\):\n     \\[\n     (-1)^4 - 80 \\cdot (-1) - 36 = 1 + 80 - 36 = 45 \\quad \\text{(not a root)}\n     \\]\n   - For \\(x = 2\\):\n     \\[\n     2^4 - 80 \\cdot 2 - 36 = 16 - 160 - 36 = -180 \\quad \\text{(not a root)}\n     \\]\n   - For \\(x = -2\\):\n     \\[\n     (-2)^4 - 80 \\cdot (-2) - 36 = 16 + 160 - 36 = 140 \\quad \\text{(not a root)}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Use Intermediate Value Theorem**: Since direct testing did not reveal any roots, we use the Intermediate Value Theorem to check for sign changes to ensure there are real roots.\n   - Evaluate at \\(x = 4\\):\n     \\[\n     4^4 - 80 \\cdot 4 - 36 = 256 - 320 - 36 = -100 \\quad \\text{(negative)}\n     \\]\n   - Evaluate at \\(x = 5\\):\n     \\[\n     5^4 - 80 \\cdot 5 - 36 = 625 - 400 - 36 = 189 \\quad \\text{(positive)}\n     \\]\n   Since the function changes sign between \\(x = 4\\) and \\(x = 5\\), there is at least one real root in this interval.\n\n5. **Sum of Real Roots Using Symmetry and Polynomial Properties**: Since the polynomial is of degree 4 and does not have any obvious symmetry (like even or odd function properties), we need to consider the sum of all roots. By Vieta's formulas, the sum of all roots (real and complex) of \\(x^4 - 80x - 36 = 0\\) is zero (since the coefficient of \\(x^3\\) is zero).\n\n6. **Sum of Real Roots**: Since the polynomial does not have any apparent symmetry and we have identified at least one real root, let's denote the real roots as \\(r_1\\) and \\(r_2\\). The sum of all four roots is zero, and thus the sum of the complex roots (if any) will be the negative of the sum of the real roots. If there are no complex roots or they sum to zero, the sum of the real roots is still zero.\n\nTherefore, the sum of the real roots of the polynomial \\(x^4 - 80x - 36 = 0\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{0}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
